---
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="Privacy Policy" description="How LiteMCP Composer processes design-partner applications and, for invited users, managed-cloud account, configuration, and operational data.">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Legal · Design-partner and managed preview"
    title="Privacy Policy"
    description="This policy describes the public website, design-partner application, and invite-only hosted preview. A self-hosted operator controls its own deployment and data practices."
    signal="Effective 22 July 2026"
  >
    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <a class="button button--primary button--large" href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>
      <a class="button button--large" href="/security">Security model</a>
    </Fragment>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          This Privacy Policy applies to the LiteMCP Composer website, design-partner intake, and
          invite-only managed-cloud preview (the “Service”). It does not control a self-hosted
          installation or an upstream MCP server you choose to connect. The LiteMCP Composer
          project maintainers operate the current preview; a formal commercial operator and
          address have not been announced. The Service must not represent general availability
          until that controller information and production retention terms are published.
        </p>

        <h2 id="data">1. Information we process</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Account data:</strong> name, email address, verification state, password credential hash, sessions, authentication factors, and recovery records.</li>
          <li><strong>Organization data:</strong> memberships, roles, invitations, identity-provider metadata, API-key metadata, and administrator actions.</li>
          <li><strong>Design-partner application:</strong> work contact, organization and role, coarse server and user ranges, MCP clients, upstream-authentication and identity-provider categories, deployment preference, timeline, proposed sensitive workflow, consent, and privacy-notice version.</li>
          <li><strong>Product configuration:</strong> MCP server definitions, compositions, environments, policies, approvals, session metadata, and configuration exports.</li>
          <li><strong>Operational data:</strong> request identifiers, timestamps, route and outcome data, IP address and user-agent information received by authentication or infrastructure layers, rate-limit counters, security events, and audit records.</li>
          <li><strong>Communications:</strong> email delivery metadata and information you include in support, security, or design-partner messages.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          MCP tool arguments and responses necessarily pass between your client and the upstream
          server during execution. The product is designed to avoid storing their plaintext in
          audit records; approval records use an arguments hash. Infrastructure and upstream
          behavior still require production validation, so do not send sensitive payloads to the
          managed preview.
        </p>

        <h2 id="purpose">2. Why we use information</h2>
        <p>We process information to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>create and secure accounts, sessions, organizations, and invitations;</li>
          <li>provide composition, policy, routing, approval, audit, and export features;</li>
          <li>deliver verification, password-reset, invitation, and service emails;</li>
          <li>qualify and coordinate a requested design-partner pilot;</li>
          <li>prevent abuse, enforce rate limits, troubleshoot failures, and protect tenants;</li>
          <li>understand preview activation and improve reliability; and</li>
          <li>comply with law and respond to valid legal requests.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Where data-protection law applies, the relevant bases are performing the service you
          request, legitimate interests in operating and securing the preview, compliance with
          legal obligations, and consent where specifically requested. We do not sell personal
          information or use it for third-party behavioral advertising.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sharing">3. Service providers and disclosure</h2>
        <p>
          The current managed-cloud composition uses Cloudflare for website delivery, Worker
          execution, and KV/D1 storage. Resend is used when transactional email is enabled. These
          providers process data on our behalf under their own infrastructure and contractual
          controls. Tool-call data is also sent to the upstream MCP server and network provider
          selected by your organization.
        </p>
        <p>
          We may disclose information when required by law, to protect rights and security, or as
          part of a future organizational transfer with appropriate notice and safeguards. We do
          not disclose one tenant's data to another tenant.
        </p>

        <h2 id="international">4. International processing</h2>
        <p>
          Infrastructure providers may process data in countries other than yours. Before the
          Service accepts production customers, regional availability, transfer mechanisms, and
          processor terms must be published. The preview makes no data-residency commitment.
        </p>

        <h2 id="retention">5. Retention and deletion</h2>
        <p>
          New design-partner applications expire after 180 days and are deleted on the next daily
          private-data cleanup pass. Under routine operation that adds no more than one day of
          deletion lag, but it is not an external retention SLA. The intake uses a keyed network
          hash for hourly abuse limits, does not join it to the application, and expires that abuse
          bucket after 48 hours under the same daily cleanup schedule. It does not retain the raw
          network address in the application record.
        </p>
        <p>
          We keep account and configuration data while the account or organization is active and
          as needed for security, backup, dispute, and legal purposes. The preview does not yet
          claim a validated fixed retention or backup-deletion schedule. This is a release blocker,
          not permission to retain data indefinitely: deletion requests will be handled manually
          while automated lifecycle controls are completed.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cookies">6. Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          The Service uses necessary authentication and security cookies. It does not currently
          advertise marketing cookies or third-party behavioral tracking. If optional analytics or
          other non-essential cookies are introduced, this policy and any required consent controls
          will be updated first.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rights">7. Your choices and rights</h2>
        <p>
          Depending on where you live, you may request access, correction, export, deletion,
          restriction, or objection; withdraw consent; or complain to a data-protection authority.
          Organization administrators may control some records first. We may need to verify your
          identity and retain limited information where law or security requires it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Send a privacy request to
          <a href="mailto:enterprise@litemcpcomposer.com?subject=LiteMCP%20Composer%20privacy%20request">enterprise@litemcpcomposer.com</a>
          with “privacy request” in the subject. Do not include passwords, API keys, tokens, or
          sensitive tool payloads in email.
        </p>

        <h2 id="security">8. Security and children</h2>
        <p>
          Security controls and current limitations are described on the <a href="/security">security page</a>.
          No online service can guarantee absolute security. The Service is intended for workplace
          users and is not directed to children under 16.
        </p>

        <h2 id="changes">9. Changes</h2>
        <p>
          We will update the effective date when this policy changes. Material changes will receive
          reasonable website, in-product, or email notice when account service is available.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
